<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#arras-culture">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arras culture</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 400 BCE – 1st century CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arras">Arras</a> culture is an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> culture of East <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorkshire">Yorkshire</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, from about the 4th century BCE to the Roman conquest, named after a cemetery at Arras near Market Weighton. It is marked by burials under small square barrows, some containing dismantled two-wheeled vehicles, as at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wetwang">Wetwang</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garton-slack">Garton Slack</a>. The likeness of these rites to those of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> has fuelled debate over migration, and the culture is linked to the Parisi of Roman sources.</p></div>
